select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p3-b01-033","paper":3,"topic":"Demand Side Management","kind":"fact","front":"What are the two broad types of DSM measures?","back":"(a) Energy reduction programmes, which cut demand through more efficient processes, buildings or equipment. (b) Load management programmes, which shift load away from peak times and peak rates.","explain":"Examples of (a): efficient lighting, efficient motors, preventive maintenance and energy audit. Examples of (b): load levelling, time-of-use tariffs and power-factor penalties.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p49","order":3126},
{"id":"p3-b01-034","paper":3,"topic":"Demand Side Management","kind":"fact","front":"Define peak clipping and valley filling as load-shape objectives.","back":"Peak clipping: reducing utility load mainly during peak-demand periods. Valley filling: building load in off-peak periods to improve the system load factor.","explain":"Load shifting combines the two, reducing peak load while building off-peak load, and typically leaves total electricity sales largely unchanged. Conservation, by contrast, reduces load across most hours.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p49","order":3127},
{"id":"p3-b01-035","paper":3,"topic":"Harmonics","kind":"fact","front":"What is a harmonic, and what frequency is the 5th harmonic on a 50 Hz system?","back":"A harmonic is a component frequency that is an integer multiple of the fundamental frequency. The 5th harmonic on a 50 Hz system is 250 Hz.","explain":"Harmonics come from non-linear loads. Harmonic current cannot be used by equipment on the network, so it is converted to heat and wasted, and it can travel back into the supply.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p50","order":3128},
{"id":"p3-b01-036","paper":3,"topic":"Harmonics","kind":"problem","front":"The fundamental current is 250 A, the 3rd harmonic current is 50 A and the 5th harmonic current is 35 A. What is the current THD?","back":"About 24%.","explain":"Current THD is the root-sum-square of the harmonic currents divided by the fundamental. A simple linear sum would give 34%, so the RSS form is the one that matches the chapter's result.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p51","steps":[{"prompt":"Square each harmonic current.","answer":"50^2 = 2500; 35^2 = 1225.","explain":"Harmonic currents are combined on a root-sum-square basis."},{"prompt":"Add the squares and take the square root.","answer":"sqrt(2500 + 1225) = sqrt(3725) = about 61.0 A.","explain":"This is the RMS value of the harmonic content."},{"prompt":"Divide by the fundamental current.","answer":"61.0 / 250 = about 0.244.","explain":"THD is measured relative to the fundamental (base) current."},{"prompt":"Express as a percentage.","answer":"THD = about 24%.","explain":"This matches the chapter's worked result."}],"order":3129},
{"id":"p3-b01-037","paper":3,"topic":"Harmonics","kind":"fact","front":"What is the individual odd-harmonic current limit (h below 11) and the TDD limit for a system with Isc/IL below 20?","back":"Individual limit 4.0% of IL for h below 11; total demand distortion (TDD) limit 5.0%.","explain":"Even harmonics are limited to 25% of the odd-harmonic limits. Distortion that causes a DC offset, such as from half-wave converters, is not allowed. Limits apply at the point of common coupling.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p54","order":3130},
{"id":"p3-b01-038","paper":3,"topic":"Harmonics","kind":"fact","front":"What advantages does an active front-end (AFE) rectifier in a VFD offer for harmonics?","back":"It mitigates harmonics without external filters, keeps unity power factor at the point of common coupling, allows energy to flow back to the mains, and holds the DC-link voltage steady despite supply variations.","explain":"An LCL filter between the grid and the AFE bypasses the high-frequency switching harmonics, so the grid sees a near-sinusoidal supply.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p54","order":3131},
{"id":"p3-b01-039","paper":3,"topic":"Harmonics","kind":"fact","front":"Who is responsible for limiting current distortion, and who for voltage distortion?","back":"The customer limits current distortion; the utility limits voltage distortion.","explain":"Limits apply at the point of common coupling (PCC), usually the utility ownership or metering point, so the whole plant is judged there rather than individual panels or equipment.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p55","order":3132},
{"id":"p3-b01-040","paper":3,"topic":"Electrical System Analysis","kind":"fact","front":"Which tool is used to find loose or corroded connections and hot spots in distribution equipment?","back":"An infra-red (IR) camera.","explain":"Poor connections raise resistance, causing hot spots, voltage drops and energy waste. Such checks support reliability-centred maintenance, which the chapter notes can save money, energy and downtime.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p56","order":3133},
{"id":"p3-b01-041","paper":3,"topic":"Maximum Demand","kind":"problem","front":"A plant has a 5000 kVA contract demand (minimum billable 75%) and a recorded MD of 3850 kVA at PF 0.95. MD is billed at Rs 600/kVA/month. Each 0.01 PF rise above 0.95 cuts the energy charge by 0.5%; energy charges average Rs 18 lakh/month. Find the capacitor kVAr to reach unity PF, the annual saving, and the simple payback at Rs 900/kVAr.","back":"About 1203 kVAr (cost Rs 10,82,700). Annual saving Rs 12,60,000 (MD Rs 7,20,000 + energy Rs 5,40,000). Payback about 0.86 years (about 10.3 months).","explain":"The MD saving is limited because billing stays at the 75% floor (3750 kVA), not the new lower demand. The energy saving comes from the PF incentive: 0.95 to 1.00 is five steps of 0.01, i.e. 2.5%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p57","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the kW drawn.","answer":"3850 x 0.95 = 3657.5 kW.","explain":"kW = kVA x PF."},{"prompt":"Find the kVAr needed to go from 0.95 to unity PF.","answer":"kVAr = 3657.5 x (tan phi1 - tan phi2) = 3657.5 x (0.329 - 0) = about 1203 kVAr.","explain":"Unity PF means the reactive part is fully supplied locally."},{"prompt":"Find the capacitor cost.","answer":"1203 x Rs 900 = Rs 10,82,700.","explain":"This is the investment for the payback calculation."},{"prompt":"Find the annual MD saving.","answer":"Billed MD falls from 3850 to the 75% floor of 3750 kVA: 100 kVA x 12 x Rs 600 = Rs 7,20,000 per year.","explain":"Unity PF gives 3657.5 kVA, but billing remains at 3750 kVA."},{"prompt":"Find the annual energy-charge saving.","answer":"2.5% x Rs 18,00,000 = Rs 45,000 per month; x 12 = Rs 5,40,000 per year.","explain":"Five steps of 0.01 above 0.95 at 0.5% each give 2.5%."},{"prompt":"Find the simple payback.","answer":"Total saving = Rs 12,60,000 per year; payback = 10,82,700 / 12,60,000 = about 0.86 years (about 10.3 months).","explain":"Simple payback ignores financing and time value of money."}],"order":3134},
{"id":"p3-b01-042","paper":3,"topic":"Maximum Demand","kind":"problem","front":"A plant with a 5000 kVA contract demand (75% minimum billing) has recorded MD of 3850 kVA. Correcting PF to unity reduces the MD to 3657.5 kVA. How much billable MD is actually saved per month at Rs 600/kVA?","back":"100 kVA, i.e. Rs 60,000 per month. Not the 192.5 kVA reduction in recorded demand.","explain":"Billing uses the higher of recorded demand and the 75% minimum billable demand, so the saving stops at 3750 kVA.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p57","steps":[{"prompt":"Compute the minimum billable demand.","answer":"0.75 x 5000 = 3750 kVA.","explain":"The utility bills on at least this figure."},{"prompt":"Compute the billable MD before correction.","answer":"max(3850, 3750) = 3850 kVA.","explain":"Recorded demand is above the floor, so it is billed."},{"prompt":"Compute the billable MD after correction.","answer":"max(3657.5, 3750) = 3750 kVA.","explain":"The floor now applies."},{"prompt":"Find the saving.","answer":"3850 - 3750 = 100 kVA; x Rs 600 = Rs 60,000 per month.","explain":"The unit saving is limited by the minimum billing clause."}],"order":3135},
{"id":"p3-b01-043","paper":3,"topic":"Power Factor","kind":"problem","front":"A load of 500 kW at PF 0.85 is to be raised to PF 0.95. What kVAr of capacitors is required?","back":"About 145 kVAr (option a in the chapter's objective questions).","explain":"This is Question 2 of the chapter's objective-type questions, solved with kVAr = kW x (tan phi1 - tan phi2).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p58","steps":[{"prompt":"Find tan phi1 for PF 0.85.","answer":"tan(cos^-1 0.85) = about 0.620.","explain":"Present power factor."},{"prompt":"Find tan phi2 for PF 0.95.","answer":"tan(cos^-1 0.95) = about 0.329.","explain":"Target power factor."},{"prompt":"Find the difference.","answer":"0.620 - 0.329 = 0.291.","explain":"This is the multiplier per kW."},{"prompt":"Multiply by the kW load.","answer":"500 x 0.291 = about 145 kVAr.","explain":"Matches option a."}],"order":3136},
{"id":"p3-b01-044","paper":3,"topic":"Capacitors","kind":"problem","front":"A 415 V capacitor has its applied voltage reduced by 10%. By how much does its kVAr output fall?","back":"About 19%, because kVAr output scales with the voltage squared (0.9^2 = 0.81).","explain":"The chapter's voltage-effects rule: output equals rated kVAr x (actual voltage / rated voltage) squared. A 10% fall in voltage therefore cuts output by about 19%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 01 p58","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the voltage ratio.","answer":"V_actual / V_rated = 0.90.","explain":"A 10% reduction gives a ratio of 0.90."},{"prompt":"Square the ratio.","answer":"0.90^2 = 0.81.","explain":"Reactive output scales with voltage squared."},{"prompt":"Find the fall in output.","answer":"1 - 0.81 = 0.19, i.e. 19%.","explain":"This is the reduction in kVAr output."},{"prompt":"Match to the option.","answer":"Option b (19%).","explain":"Options a, c and d are 23%, 87% and 10%."}],"order":3137},
{"id":"p3-b02-001","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What four operating components are common to all electric motors?","back":"Stator (stationary windings), rotor (rotating windings), bearings and frame (enclosure).","explain":"These four parts are present in induction, DC and synchronous motors alike. The motor types differ mainly in how the windings are energised and arranged.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p63","order":3138},
{"id":"p3-b02-002","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Why does an induction motor rotor always run slower than the stator's rotating field?","back":"Relative motion between the rotating flux and the rotor conductors is needed to induce rotor current. Lenz's law makes the rotor current oppose that relative motion, so the rotor chases the field but never catches it.","explain":"The EMF induced in the rotor has the same frequency as the supply. If the rotor matched the field speed there would be no relative motion, no induced current and no torque.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p63","order":3139},
{"id":"p3-b02-003","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"How does a slip-ring (wound-rotor) motor differ from a squirrel cage motor?","back":"Its rotor is wound with coils whose ends go to slip rings. Carbon brushes on the rings let external resistors or contactors be added in series with the rotor.","explain":"Extra rotor resistance moves the pull-out torque to a lower speed, so the motor gives high torque at low starting current. The resistance is cut out once the motor reaches base speed, which suits very high inertia loads.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p64","order":3140},
{"id":"p3-b02-004","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Down to what speed can rotor resistors slow a slip-ring motor at full load before efficiency falls sharply?","back":"About 50% of synchronous speed. Below that, power dissipated in the rotor resistances makes efficiency very low.","explain":"Slip-ring motors suit variable torque, variable speed loads such as printing presses, compressors, conveyor belts, hoists and elevators. Rotor-resistance speed control wastes energy as heat, so it is best kept above about 50% speed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p64","order":3141},
{"id":"p3-b02-005","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"How is the rotor of a synchronous motor energised, and how does its speed compare with the supply?","back":"A separate DC source feeds the rotor. It locks onto the stator's rotating field and runs at synchronous speed with no slip.","explain":"Synchronous speed depends only on supply frequency and the number of poles. Because there is no slip, the DC excitation supplies the energy that slip would otherwise provide in an induction motor.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p64","order":3142},
{"id":"p3-b02-006","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Which pole counts does the chapter list for motors on 50 Hz mains?","back":"2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 and 16 poles. The pole count is always even.","explain":"Synchronous speed (rpm) = 120 × frequency (Hz) / number of poles, so a 4-pole motor on 50 Hz runs at 120 × 50 / 4 = 1500 rpm. The pole count is fixed at construction, so speed can only be changed by frequency (VFD) or a different winding.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p65","order":3143},
{"id":"p3-b02-007","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"How is motor slip (%) defined?","back":"Slip (%) = (Synchronous speed − full-load rated speed) / Synchronous speed × 100.","explain":"Slip is the speed gap that lets the rotor induce current and produce torque. The chapter notes that nameplate full-load slip is not accurate, so measuring slip under full load gives better results.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p65","order":3144},
{"id":"p3-b02-008","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Why are induction motors the main reason for low power factor in electrical systems?","back":"Induction motors are inductive loads with PF below one, and their power factor drops as the load falls. Motors running well below rated capacity therefore pull the plant PF down.","explain":"As load comes down, the active current reduces and the motor PF reduces with it. Oversized or lightly loaded motors are the usual cause of this problem.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p66","order":3145},
{"id":"p3-b02-009","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"How is motor efficiency defined?","back":"Mechanical energy delivered at the rotating shaft divided by the electrical energy input at the motor terminals.","explain":"Because motor PF is below one, more current is needed for the same real power, and upstream wiring losses rise with the square of current. A high efficiency and a PF close to unity are both wanted for efficient plant operation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p66","order":3146},
{"id":"p3-b02-010","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Which motors are more efficient: squirrel cage or slip-ring, TEFC or SPDP, higher speed or lower speed, larger or smaller rating?","back":"Squirrel cage beats slip-ring; higher-speed motors beat lower-speed ones; TEFC beats SPDP; and efficiency rises with rated capacity.","explain":"Efficiency also depends on motor temperature. TEFC means totally enclosed, fan-cooled, and SPDP means screen-protected, drip-proof.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p66","order":3147},
{"id":"p3-b02-011","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Which motor losses are fixed (load-independent) and which are variable (load-dependent)?","back":"Fixed: magnetic core (iron) losses and friction and windage. Variable: stator and rotor resistance (I²R) losses and stray load losses.","explain":"Core losses are eddy current and hysteresis losses in the stator steel. Stray losses vary roughly with the square of the rotor current and are hard to measure directly. Intrinsic losses can only be cut by changing the motor design.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p66","order":3148},
{"id":"p3-b02-012","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What stray-load loss does IEEE 112 assign to a 300 HP motor?","back":"1.5%, from the 125 to 500 HP band. The other bands are: 1 to 125 HP 1.8%; 501 to 2499 HP 1.2%; 2500 HP and above 0.9%.","explain":"IEEE 112 is the detailed method and is rarely used on the shop floor. IS and IEC standards use a flat 0.5% of input instead.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p68","order":3149},
{"id":"p3-b02-013","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What stray-load loss do IS and IEC motor standards assume, and how does the actual value compare?","back":"A fixed 0.5% of input power. The actual stray loss is likely higher, about 1 to 3% for motors up to 200 HP.","explain":"Stray losses are hard to measure, so the standard value understates total losses. This is why efficiency figures based on IEEE methods come out lower.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p68","order":3150},
{"id":"p3-b02-014","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Can two test methods, or two manufacturers, give different efficiency figures for the same motor?","back":"Yes. The same motor can differ by about 2% between test methods, or between manufacturers using the same method.","explain":"The chapter advises asking for detailed test certificates, specifying efficiency without tolerance, and relying on measured input current and kW rather than labelled values.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p68","order":3151},
{"id":"p3-b02-015","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"How is rotor I²R loss estimated in the field?","back":"Rotor I²R loss = slip × (stator input − stator I²R loss − core loss).","explain":"Slip is measured with a stroboscope or non-contact tachometer. The chapter also requires the slip to be corrected to operating temperature.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p68","order":3152},
{"id":"p3-b02-016","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"problem","front":"A 34 kW, 415 V, 57 A, 1475 rpm delta motor has stator resistance 0.264 Ω per phase at 30 °C. Its no-load test gives 415 V, 16.1 A and 1063.74 W. Estimate its full-load efficiency and full-load power factor.","back":"Full-load efficiency about 92.2%; full-load PF about 0.90. Iron plus friction and windage about 995 W; stator I²R at 120 °C about 1150 W; slip 1.67%; motor input about 36.9 kW.","explain":"This is the chapter's field estimation method, which uses measured values for every calculation. Nameplate efficiency is used only for comparison.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p69","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the iron plus friction and windage losses at no load.","answer":"Stator copper loss at 30 °C = 3 × (16.1/√3)² × 0.264 = 68.43 W. Iron + F&W = 1063.74 − 68.43 = 995.3 W.","explain":"At no load the input supplies only stator I²R, core loss and friction and windage, so subtracting the stator copper loss leaves the fixed losses."},{"prompt":"Correct the stator resistance from 30 °C to 120 °C.","answer":"R₁₂₀ = 0.264 × (120 + 235)/(30 + 235) = 0.354 Ω per phase.","explain":"Modern motors run at 100 to 120 °C, so the 30 °C reading must be corrected upward before running losses are calculated."},{"prompt":"Find the full-load stator copper loss at 120 °C.","answer":"3 × (57/√3)² × 0.354 = 1150.1 W.","explain":"Stator I²R uses the hot resistance and the full-load line current."},{"prompt":"Find the full-load slip and the rotor input.","answer":"Slip = (1500 − 1475)/1500 = 0.0167. Rotor input = 34000/(1 − 0.0167) = 34577.4 W.","explain":"Rotor input is shaft output divided by (1 − slip), because rotor losses equal slip times rotor input."},{"prompt":"Add the stray losses (0.5% of rated output) to get the motor input.","answer":"P_in = 34577.4 + 1150.1 + 995.3 + 170 (0.5% of 34000) = 36892.8 W.","explain":"Standards assume stray losses of 0.5%. The chapter notes actual values are often 1 to 3%, so this estimate is optimistic."},{"prompt":"Calculate the full-load efficiency and power factor.","answer":"Efficiency = 34000/36892.8 = 92.2%. PF = 36892.8/(√3 × 415 × 57) ≈ 0.90.","explain":"Power factor is real input divided by apparent input, using the measured current. The result is only as good as the test data behind it."}],"order":3153},
{"id":"p3-b02-017","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What efficiency tolerance does IS 325 allow on standard motors?","back":"15% tolerance on efficiency for motors up to 50 kW, and 10% for motors above 50 kW.","explain":"A labelled efficiency can therefore sit well below the true value. Buyers should ask for test certificates rather than rely on labelled values.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p72","order":3154},
{"id":"p3-b02-018","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Why can the cheapest motor end up costing the most?","back":"A low-cost motor usually has lower efficiency, so its running cost over life is higher. An efficient motor often saves several times its extra cost within a few years.","explain":"Buyers should also check the power drawn at 75% loading and the reactive kVAr drawn, both useful selection indicators. Being first-cost sensitive is a common reason for poor lifecycle economics.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p72","order":3155},
{"id":"p3-b02-019","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What share of total motor losses do stator and rotor I²R losses typically make up?","back":"About 55 to 60%. Core losses make up 20 to 25%, friction and windage 8 to 12%, and stray load losses 4 to 5%.","explain":"Stator I²R falls with more copper and larger conductors, and rotor I²R falls with larger or copper rotor bars. Operating closer to synchronous speed also reduces rotor losses.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p73","order":3156},
{"id":"p3-b02-020","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"How much higher is the full-load efficiency of an energy-efficient motor than that of a standard motor?","back":"About 3 to 7% higher. Mounting dimensions are kept to IS 1231 so the motor can replace the old one easily.","explain":"Energy-efficient motors use design changes such as lower-loss steel, longer cores, thicker wires, copper rotor bars and a smaller air gap. Their higher price is usually recovered quickly through operating savings.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p74","order":3157},
{"id":"p3-b02-021","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Name three situations where energy-efficient motors are generally poorly suited.","back":"Highly intermittent or special torque duties such as hoists, cranes, traction drives, punch presses, machine tools and centrifuges; applications needing multi-speed motors; and duties where the motor runs only on continuous duty cycles.","explain":"Their economics depend on savings from running hours, so they pay back least where the motor runs rarely. They are most attractive in new applications or at end-of-life replacement.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p74","order":3158},
{"id":"p3-b02-022","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What service factor and design loading does the chapter recommend for energy-efficient motors?","back":"A 1.15 service factor, with the motor designed to operate at about 85% of rated load.","explain":"Poor incoming power quality can affect energy-efficient motors. They also run about 1% faster than standard motors because of their lower slip.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p75","order":3159},
{"id":"p3-b02-023","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What supply variation must a standard Indian motor tolerate under BIS?","back":"It must deliver rated output with ±6% voltage variation and ±3% frequency variation.","explain":"Fluctuations larger than these are common in utility supply in India, and voltage fluctuations affect motor performance. Voltage unbalance is a separate issue, recommended at 1% or less.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p75","order":3160},
{"id":"p3-b02-024","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What is the recommended maximum voltage unbalance at motor terminals?","back":"1%. Above this, the motor must be derated.","explain":"Voltage unbalance is probably the leading power factor problem behind motor overheating and premature failure. Current imbalance can be 6 to 10 times the voltage imbalance.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p77","order":3161},
{"id":"p3-b02-025","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"problem","front":"Line voltages measured at a motor supply are 410 V, 417 V and 408 V. What is the NEMA percentage voltage unbalance?","back":"About 1.29%, which is above the 1% recommended limit, so the motor would need derating.","explain":"NEMA defines unbalance from the mean of the three line voltages and the largest single deviation. Unequal single-phase loads and unequal cable impedance are common causes.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p77","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the mean of the three line voltages.","answer":"(410 + 417 + 408)/3 = 411.7 V.","explain":"NEMA measures unbalance relative to the average of the three line voltages."},{"prompt":"Find the maximum deviation from the mean.","answer":"417 − 411.7 = 5.3 V (the other deviations are 1.7 V and 3.7 V).","explain":"NEMA uses the largest single deviation, not the average deviation."},{"prompt":"Compute the percentage voltage unbalance.","answer":"5.3/411.7 × 100 = 1.29%.","explain":"This is the chapter's worked example."},{"prompt":"Compare the result with the recommended limit.","answer":"1.29% is above the 1% recommended maximum at motor terminals, so derating is needed.","explain":"Even a small unbalance heats the motor noticeably, as the next card shows."}],"order":3162},
{"id":"p3-b02-026","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"problem","front":"A motor running at 100 °C has a voltage unbalance of 1.29%. Estimate the additional temperature rise.","back":"About 3.3 °C, using additional temperature rise = 2 × (% voltage unbalance)².","explain":"The chapter's own example uses a 2% unbalance at 100 °C, which gives an 8 °C rise. Insulation life halves for each 10 °C rise, so even a small unbalance matters.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p78","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the rule given in the chapter.","answer":"Additional temperature rise (°C) = 2 × (% voltage unbalance)².","explain":"The rule is the chapter's empirical estimate for motor heating under voltage unbalance."},{"prompt":"Substitute the unbalance of 1.29%.","answer":"2 × 1.29² = 2 × 1.664 = 3.33 °C.","explain":"Because the rise depends on the square of the unbalance, heating grows quickly as unbalance increases."},{"prompt":"Find the new operating temperature.","answer":"100 + 3.3 ≈ 103.3 °C.","explain":"Insulation life is halved for each 10 °C increase in operating temperature, so this extra heat shortens motor life."}],"order":3163},
{"id":"p3-b02-027","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"How is percentage motor loading estimated, and what is the common mistake?","back":"% loading = measured input kW ÷ (rated kW ÷ rated efficiency) × 100. Never assume the power factor, and do not estimate loading as the ratio of currents.","explain":"The current ratio ignores changes in power factor and efficiency at part load. Measuring kW input directly gives a reliable loading figure.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p78","order":3164},
{"id":"p3-b02-028","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Why is under-loading a common cause of poor motor efficiency?","back":"It lowers both efficiency and power factor and raises first cost. Replacing motors running at 60 to 70% of capacity or higher is generally not recommended.","explain":"Causes include large OEM safety factors, equipment that rarely needs full capacity, oversizing to hold output at low voltage, and oversizing for high starting torque. Each case needs its own evaluation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p80","order":3165},
{"id":"p3-b02-029","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"How does the chapter suggest choosing a motor rating for a variable load?","back":"Use the load duration curve: pick a rating whose continuous full-load temperature rise equals the weighted average temperature rise over the cycle. A rating slightly below peak load can then run briefly overloaded.","explain":"This method underestimates heating for frequent starts and stops or high-inertia loads, so it should not be used for those duties.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p81","order":3166},
{"id":"p3-b02-030","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What is the maximum capacitor size that should be connected across a motor?","back":"No more than 90% of the motor's no-load kVAr. Larger capacitors can cause over-voltage and motor burn-out.","explain":"No-load kVAr is found from a no-load test. If testing is not possible, standard power factor values for typical motors give a conservative estimate.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p81","order":3167},
{"id":"p3-b02-031","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Does a power factor capacitor fitted at a motor's starter improve the motor's own operating power factor?","back":"No. It improves the power factor upstream, from the capacitor back to the generating side, not the motor's own operating PF.","explain":"Upstream benefits include lower kVA demand, lower I²R losses in cables and reduced voltage drop. The largest overall system gain comes when the capacitor is across the motor terminals.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p81","order":3168}
]$cards$::jsonb);
